## Claim
Text: "A crowdfunding campaign on the Christian site GiveSendGo solicited donations to help Kyle Rittenhouse, a 17-year-old accused of killing two people at a protest in Kenosha, Wisconsin."
Claim author: Cassandra of Troy
Claim date: August 28, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Was a crowdfunding campaign for Kyle Rittenhouse hosted on the website GiveSendGo in August 2020?
Answer: Yes, a crowdfunding campaign for Kyle Rittenhouse was hosted on the Christian website GiveSendGo in August 2020, and as of the reporting, it had raised over $97,000. The source states, "The Christian site GiveSendGo has no plans to take down their campaign in support of Kyle Rittenhouse" and notes that while other platforms deactivated similar campaigns, GiveSendGo kept this one active.

Source URL: https://news.yahoo.com/christian-raises-over-97k-kenosha-211803698.html

### Is GiveSendGo a website that identifies itself as a Christian crowdfunding platform?
Answer: Yes, GiveSendGo identifies itself as a Christian crowdfunding platform, as it is described in the source as "a free Christian alternative to many other crowdfunding sites."

Source URL: https://www.alignable.com/dover-de/givesendgo-llc

### What were the stated goals or descriptions within the Kyle Rittenhouse crowdfunding campaign on GiveSendGo?
Answer: The Kyle Rittenhouse crowdfunding campaign on GiveSendGo stated that its goal was to support him for "clearly self-defense," arguing he was "unfairly charged" with murder after defending himself "from a brutal attack by multiple members of the far-leftist group ANTIFA."

Source URL: https://news.yahoo.com/christian-raises-over-97k-kenosha-211803698.html

### How much money did the Kyle Rittenhouse crowdfunding campaign on GiveSendGo raise?
Answer: The request was rejected because it was considered high risk

