## Final Judgement
The fact-check evidence confirms the core elements of the claim: a crowdfunding campaign for Kyle Rittenhouse was hosted on the self-described Christian site GiveSendGo in August 2020. The campaign solicited donations to support him for what it argued was self-defense. The evidence also verifies that Rittenhouse was 17 years old at the time, he was accused of killing two people during a protest in Kenosha, Wisconsin, and he was officially charged with multiple felonies, including homicide. All factual components of the claim are directly corroborated by the sourced information.

The evidence is clear and consistent, providing no need for further investigation on any part of the claim.

Based on the recorded evidence, every factual assertion in the claim is verified. The crowdfunding campaign existed on the Christian site GiveSendGo, it was for Kyle Rittenhouse, he was 17 at the time, and he was accused of killing two people at a protest. Therefore, the knowledge from the fact-check supports the claim. `supported`

### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
The fact-check confirms that a crowdfunding campaign for Kyle Rittenhouse was active on the self-described Christian platform [GiveSendGo](https://news.yahoo.com/christian-raises-over-97k-kenosha-211803698.html) in August 2020, soliciting donations to support him for what it argued was self-defense. Evidence verifies Rittenhouse was 17 at the time of the [Kenosha, Wisconsin, protest](https://www.wuwm.com/regional/2020-08-28/aug-28-recall-campaign-launched-against-evers-blake-no-longer-handcuffed-in-hospital) on August 25, 2020, where he was accused of killing [two people](https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2020/8/27/what-we-know-about-the-victims-of-the-kenosha-shooting) and was later [charged with multiple felonies](https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/suspect-jacob-blake-protest-shooting-appear-court-extradition-hearing-n1238502), including homicide. All factual elements of the claim are directly corroborated by the sourced documentation.